   "id": "0c1c845a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Task 1.3: Summary of Packet vs Flow Level Features\n",
    "\n",
    "**Packet-Level Features** are those that can be derived from the headers or payload of a **single** packet, without any historical context or state.\n",
    "*   **Examples**: `Destination Port` (TCP Header), `Protocol` (IP Header), `Packet Length` (IP Header), `Flags` (TCP Header) *of a specific packet*.\n",
    "*   **Characteristics**: Extremely fast to extract (O(1)).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Flow-Level (Derived/Aggregated) Features** are calculated by tracking statistics over a sequence of packets.\n",
    "*   **Clarification on 'Packet Length Max'**: Even though `Fwd Packet Length Max` has \"Packet\" in the name, it is a **Flow** feature. Why? Because to know the \"Max\", you must observe *all* packets in the flow (or a window) to find the largest one. A single packet cannot know if it is the \"Max\" of the whole stream.\n",
    "*   **Examples**: `Flow Duration`, `Total Fwd Packets`, `Fwd Packet Length Max/Mean/Std`.\n",
    "*   **Characteristics**: Require state maintenance (hash tables) and memory.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f6677963",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Task 2 — Identify Scapy-Computable Features\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.1 Feature Selection Overview\n",
    "From the initial **79 raw features** provided in the dataset, we have identified subsets that can be computed in real-time using Scapy. The selection is divided into two profiles to account for hardware constraints:\n",
    "* **Linux Server Profile (52 features):** Includes statistical features (Standard Deviation, Variance) and complex timing metrics (IAT).\n",
    "* **Raspberry Pi Profile (23 features):** Focuses on lightweight counters, packet lengths (Max/Min), and TCP flags to ensure stability on limited hardware.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.2 Table of Computable Features\n",
    "\n",
    "The table below classifies the features and justifies their computability across both platforms.\n",
    "\n",
    "| # | Feature Name | Linux | Pi | Reason (Scope, Cost, Hardware) | Validation Snippet |\n",
    "|:---|:---|:---:|:---:|:---|:---|\n",
    "| 1 | **Destination Port** | Yes | Yes | Header extraction. | `pkt.dport` |\n",
    "| 2 | **Flow Duration** | Yes | Yes | Timestamp delta. | `now - start` |\n",
    "| 3-4 | **Total Fwd/Bwd Packets** | Yes | Yes | Simple counters. | `count += 1` |\n",
    "| 5-6 | **Total Length of Fwd/Bwd Pkts** | Yes | Yes | Cumulative sum of lengths. | `total += len(pkt)` |\n",
    "| 7-10 | **Fwd/Bwd Pkt Len Max/Min** | Yes | Yes | Comparison logic. | `max(val, old)` |\n",
    "| 11-14 | **Fwd/Bwd Pkt Len Mean/Std** | Yes | **No** | **CPU Cost:** Std Dev requires sqrt/squares. | `np.std(list)` |\n",
    "| 15-16 | **Flow Bytes/s & Pkts/s** | Yes | **No** | **Floating point:** Continuous division. | `bytes / dur` |\n",
    "| 17-20 | **Flow IAT Mean/Std/Max/Min** | Yes | **No** | **RAM:** High-res timing per flow. | `pkt.time - last` |\n",
    "| 21-25 | **Fwd IAT Tot/Mean/Std/Max/Min** | Yes | **No** | **Memory:** Directional state tracking. | `fwd_iat.mean()` |\n",
    "| 26-30 | **Bwd IAT Tot/Mean/Std/Max/Min** | Yes | **No** | **Memory:** Directional state tracking. | `bwd_iat.mean()` |\n",
    "| 31-34 | **Fwd/Bwd PSH/URG Flags** | Yes | Yes | TCP Header bitmask. | `pkt[TCP].flags` |\n",
    "| 35-36 | **Fwd/Bwd Header Length** | Yes | Yes | Header field extraction. | `pkt[TCP].dataofs` |\n",
    "| 37-38 | **Fwd/Bwd Packets/s** | Yes | **No** | Throughput calculation overhead. | `count / dur` |\n",
    "| 39-40 | **Min/Max Packet Length** | Yes | Yes | Global flow comparison. | `min(all_pkts)` |\n",
    "| 41-43 | **Pkt Len Mean/Std/Variance** | Yes | **No** | **Hardware:** Pi lacks AVX for fast stats. | `np.var(list)` |\n",
    "| 44-51 | **Flag Counts (FIN to ECE)** | Yes | Yes | Bitwise inspection (8 different flags). | `flags & 0x01` |\n",
    "| 52 | **Down/Up Ratio** | Yes | Yes | Simple division at flow end. | `bwd / fwd` |\n",
    "| 53 | **Average Packet Size** | Yes | Yes | Global mean (Total Len / Count). | `total / count` |\n",
    "| 54-55 | **Avg Fwd/Bwd Segment Size** | Yes | **No** | Redundant floating-point cost. | `sum / count` |\n",
    "| 56 | **Fwd Header Length.1** | No | No | **Redundant:** Duplicate of feature #35. | `N/A` |\n",
    "| 57-59 | **Fwd Avg Bytes/Pkts/Bulk Rate** | Yes | **No** | **Complexity:** Bulk state machine. | `bulk_logic` |\n",
    "| 60-62 | **Bwd Avg Bytes/Pkts/Bulk Rate** | Yes | **No** | **Complexity:** Bulk state machine. | `bulk_logic` |\n",
    "| 63-66 | **Subflow Fwd/Bwd Pkts/Bytes** | Yes | **No** | **RAM:** Requires flow segmentation. | `subflow_map` |\n",
    "| 67-68 | **Init_Win_bytes_fwd/bwd** | Yes | Yes | TCP Window field (SYN/ACK). | `pkt[TCP].window` |\n",
    "| 69 | **act_data_pkt_fwd** | Yes | Yes | Count if payload > 0. | `len(load) > 0` |\n",
    "| 70 | **min_seg_size_forward** | Yes | Yes | Minimal header check. | `min(h_lens)` |\n",
    "| 71-74 | **Active Mean/Std/Max/Min** | Yes | **No** | **Timeout logic:** Heavy state tracking. | `if gap > T: act` |\n",
    "| 75-78 | **Idle Mean/Std/Max/Min** | Yes | **No** | **Timeout logic:** Heavy state tracking. | `if gap > T: idle` |\n",
    "| 79 | **Label** | No | No | Target variable (Prediction). | `N/A` |\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.3 Justification and Platform Constraints\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Hardware and Resource Trade-offs\n",
    "The discrepancy between the **Linux Server** (52 features) and the **Raspberry Pi** (23 features) is a strategic choice based on the following constraints:\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **Computational Complexity (Processing Cost):**\n",
    "    Features such as `Packet Length Std` (Standard Deviation) or `Packet Length Variance` require second-order statistical calculations. While a standard Linux Server (x86_64) handles these via advanced vector instructions, a Raspberry Pi (ARM) may experience **packet dropping** if forced to perform these calculations on every packet during a high-bandwidth attack (e.g., DDoS).\n",
    "\n",
    "2.  **Memory Management (RAM & Scope):**\n",
    "    Tracking **Inter-Arrival Times (IAT)** for 2,800+ active flows (as seen in streaming or cloud-heavy traffic) requires maintaining large state tables in memory. The Raspberry Pi has limited memory bandwidth; therefore, we prioritize simple counters over historical timing averages to prevent system crashes or swap-latency.\n",
    "\n",
    "3.  **Real-Time Latency:**\n",
    "    On the Pi, the Ethernet controller often shares bus resources. Calculating `Flow Bytes/s` or `Flow Packets/s` in real-time requires high-precision system interrupts. Latency spikes on the Pi can lead to inaccurate measurements, so we focus on more reliable, direct header-derived features.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Security Privileges and Software Requirements\n",
    "* **Privileges:** On both platforms, Scapy requires **Root/Sudo** privileges. This is because capturing live traffic necessitates opening **Raw Sockets**, which bypasses the standard OS network stack for direct hardware access.\n",
    "* **Hardware Validation:** The Linux Server environment is assumed to have a dedicated NIC and high-frequency CPU cores, allowing for the \"Full\" feature extraction used in the original Kaggle dataset training phase.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Conclusion for Task 3 Integration\n",
    "By selecting only **23 features** for the Pi, we ensure that the real-time IDS remains responsive. The Linux Server, having more resources, can utilize the **52 features** to provide higher detection accuracy at the cost of higher power and memory consumption."
   ]
